    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. what about the element of \"uncontrollability\"? if the tenant has taken every reasonable step to mitigate the disruption but the external force is just too chaotic or erratic to manage, how does that affect the legal argument for invoking the clause?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"uncontrollability\" element is critical because force majeure generally requires an event that is not only unforeseeable but also entirely beyond the tenant's ability to prevent or control through reasonable measures. Even if a tenant has exhausted all mitigation efforts, they can still only invoke this clause if the disruption stems from a third party or natural force (like a pandemic or flood) rather than their own operational choices or foreseeable market shifts. While taking reasonable steps is a legal defense against liability, it does not typically transform a manageable commercial risk into an uncontrollable external event required for a force majeure defense to succeed.",
      "source": "target",
